<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of New Zealand</h1><p class="st-meta">1841 CE – 1907 CE · capital Auckland; later Wellington</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony founded after the Treaty of Waitangi, shaped by settler expansion and the New Zealand Wars, and first to give women the vote.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> was signed with Māori chiefs in 1840, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-tribes-of-new-zealand">United Tribes of New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and governed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> briefly as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a>. It became a separate colony in 1841 under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-hobson">William Hobson</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> as capital until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> replaced it in 1865.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Company and church settlements brought British migrants, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-constitution-act-1852">Constitution Act</a> of 1852 created a parliament and provinces. Disputes over land led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Wars from 1845 to 1872, followed by confiscations and the Native Land Court, which transferred most Māori land to settlers. Wool, gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otago">Otago</a> and, after 1882, refrigerated meat drove the economy. In 1893 it became the first self-governing country to give women the vote.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-seddon">Richard Seddon's</a> Liberal government, in office from 1893, pioneered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">old-age pensions</a>, compulsory labour arbitration and the purchase of large estates for small farmers. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> declined to join the Australian federation and in 1907 became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-new-zealand">Dominion of New Zealand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-new-zealand.jpg" alt="Colony of New Zealand" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Albert Hastings Markham Vector: Zscout370, Hugh Jass, s. File history · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Zealand.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1841 CE – 1907 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Auckland; later Wellington</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Hobson, George Grey, Richard Seddon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Presbyterianism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Māori</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Zealand Colony</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=colony-of-new-zealand" aria-label="Colony of New Zealand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9064 3505 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of New Zealand lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9384 3761L706 3218M9384 3761L9406 3709M9384 3761L8856 3661M9384 3761L4997 651M9384 3761L9268 3906"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M706 3218h0M9406 3709h0M8856 3661h0M4997 651h0M9268 3906h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9384 3761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9384 3761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Auckland. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=colony-of-new-zealand">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
